<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Günz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Günz is a river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia-bavaria">Bavarian Swabia</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, flowing north from the Alpine foothills to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunzburg">Günzburg</a>. It gave its name to the Günz glaciation, an early Ice Age cold stage named by Albrecht Penck and Eduard Brückner. Its valley held lands of the Margraviate of Burgau and of abbeys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottobeuren">Ottobeuren</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gunz.jpg" alt="Günz" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Mayer · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G%C3%BCnz6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
